<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baltimore, County Cork</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Baltimore is a coastal village in the far south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-cork">County Cork</a>, Ireland, overlooking Roaringwater Bay and the islands of Sherkin and Cape Clear. It was a stronghold of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odriscoll">O'Driscoll</a> lords, whose castle of Dún na Séad stands there. In June 1631 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbary-coast">Barbary</a> corsairs from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> raided the village and carried off more than a hundred inhabitants into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, an event known as the Sack of Baltimore.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baltimore-county-cork.jpg" alt="Baltimore, County Cork" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Masha Dunaeva Dunaevam · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baltimore_view,_2005.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Baltimore is the main village of the parish of Rathmore and the Islands, the southernmost parish in Ireland. Its English name is an anglicisation of the Irish Baile an Tí Mhóir, town of the big house, but its Irish name is that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odriscoll">O'Driscoll</a> castle, Dún na Séad, fort of the jewels, which has been restored and overlooks the town. In antiquity the site was considered a sanctuary for druids, and Irish mythology associates the name with the feast of Bealtaine.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Baltimore was a seat of the Corcu Loígde, former Kings of Tara and of Munster, and ring forts, souterrains and holy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> survive in the townland. The castle site was fortified from at least the early 13th century. The Genealogy of Corca Laidhe records that on Christmas Eve 1413 Simon Wicken, Mayor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waterford">Waterford</a>, sailed there with armed men, was invited to the Christmas festivities by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odriscoll">O'Driscolls</a> of Dunasead, and carried the family off to Waterford as prisoners.</p><p>About 1605 Sir Thomas Crooke founded an English Puritan colony on land rented from Sir Fineen O'Driscoll. The town lived on the pilchard fishery and in the early 1600s was a pirate base in which the whole population, the justices included, took part. It became a market town in 1607 and a borough in 1612, returning two members to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-house-of-commons">Irish House of Commons</a> from 1613 until it was disenfranchised by the Acts of Union 1800.</p><p>After the Sack of Baltimore in 1631, only two or three of those taken ever saw Ireland again. The survivors fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skibbereen">Skibbereen</a>, and the ruins were left almost deserted for generations. A slow recovery began in the 18th century, and the village was prospering again by the early 1800s before it suffered further losses in the Great Famine.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Baltimore is the main ferry port for Sherkin Island, Cape Clear Island and the Hundred Isles of Carbery. The white conical Baltimore Beacon, known as Lot's wife, about 15 metres high, was rebuilt in 1848 and 1849 at the harbour entrance. Lough Hyne, Ireland's first marine nature reserve, lies about 5 km away.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Baltimore, County Cork” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Corsairs from Algiers and Salé raided Iceland in 1627, and in 1631 a raid led by the Dutch renegade Jan Janszoon, known as Murat Reis, sacked <b>Baltimore</b> in Ireland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/regency-of-algiers">Regency of Algiers · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Their kingdom covered the coast and hinterland from around Clonakilty to <b>Baltimore</b> and inland toward Skibbereen.</blockquote><a href="/state/corcu-loigde">Corcu Loigde</a></li></ul></section>
